\begin_layout Title
An Itô process that is not a martingale
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset

[Thoughts dump]
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset FormulaMacro
\newcommand{\dP}{\text{d}\mathbb{P}}
{\text{d}\mathbb{P}}
\end_inset


\begin_inset FormulaMacro
\newcommand{\dW}{\text{d}W}
\end_inset


\begin_inset FormulaMacro
\newcommand{\d}{\mathrm{d}}
\end_inset

Fix a process 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(f_{t}\right)_{t}$
\end_inset

 that is almost surely square-integrable.
 We know that the Itô integral 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{t}\coloneqq\int_{0}^{t}f_{s}\dW_{s}$
\end_inset

 is a local martingale.
 The question is,
 how can it be that 
\begin_inset Formula $M_{t}$
\end_inset

 is not a true martingale?
 It is a martingale if 
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{0}^{t}f_{s}^{2}\d s$
\end_inset

 has a finite expectation,
 on top of being almost surely finite.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $dR=\frac{1}{R}\d t+\dW$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $R(0)=1$
\end_inset

 and consider the process 
\begin_inset Formula $1/R$
\end_inset

.
 By Itô formula,
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
1/R_{t}=1+\int_{0}^{t}-\frac{1}{R_{s}^{2}}\d W_{s}
\]

\end_inset

When 
\begin_inset Formula $R$
\end_inset

 gets close to zero,
 
\begin_inset Formula $1/R$
\end_inset

 explodes.
 
\begin_inset Formula $1/R$
\end_inset

 is a local martingale as an Itô integral,
 but is it a martingale?
 Stop 
\begin_inset Formula $1/R$
\end_inset

 when it hits the level 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\tau_{n}\coloneqq\inf_{R_{t}\le1/n}t.
\]

\end_inset

 The stopped process 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(1/R\right)_{t\wedge\tau_{n}}=1-\int_{0}^{t\wedge\tau_{n}}\frac{1}{R_{s}^{2}}\d W_{s}$
\end_inset

 is then a martingale because the integrand 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{R_{s}^{2}}$
\end_inset

 is bounded and so 
\begin_inset Formula $\int_{0}^{t}\left(R_{s}^{-2}\right)^{2}\d s$
\end_inset

 has a finite expectation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What breaks with the non-stopped process 
\begin_inset Formula $1/R_{t}$
\end_inset

?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Slow stopping times
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
It has always seemed to me that if a process is a martingale 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

up to a point
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 and that 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

point
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 can be arbitrary far into the future,
 then surely the process is a martingale as a whole towards infinity?
 It turns out,
 though,
 the problem is 
\emph on
the speed 
\emph default
at which this is happening.
 The domain 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is not compact,
 so we can get arbitrary slow convergence to infinity.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider for example the noncompact probability space 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega=(0,1)$
\end_inset

 with Borel sigma algebra with stopping times 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{n}(\omega)=n\omega$
\end_inset

.
 We can picture this on the infinite strip 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega\times[0,\infty)$
\end_inset

,
 each stopping time is a line through the point 
\begin_inset Formula $(0,0)$
\end_inset

 (which is not part of the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

phase space
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 because 
\begin_inset Formula $0\not\in\Omega$
\end_inset

) and that line becomes steeper and steeper as 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 goes to 
\begin_inset Formula $\infty$
\end_inset

.
 Clearly,
 at each fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega\in\Omega$
\end_inset

,
 the stopping times leak 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{n}(\omega)=n\omega\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}\infty$
\end_inset

.
 The speed at which that happens is the number 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

.
 So around 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

,
 the stopping times are incredibly slow at reaching infinity compared to how they do it away from 0.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is how it can happen that even if a process is a martingale 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

up to a moment
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 for a sequence of moments tending to 
\begin_inset Formula $\infty$
\end_inset

,
 still the process as a whole might not be a martingale at any chosen 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 How?
 For any 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 and any 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

far
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

,
 there is still a non-null event on which the process is still frozen before 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{n}$
\end_inset

 (by which I mean that 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{n}<t$
\end_inset

 is an event of positive probability),
 and defrosting it over all 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 at once might reveal explosive behaviour which distorts the expectation (which was unseen by the stopped processes).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An important prerequisite for this realization is to treat 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 and time 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 as separate dimensions,
 and to see that 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{n}$
\end_inset

 move through 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset

 and not through 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Mismatch in convergence
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The stopped processes obviously converge to the unstopped one almost surely for each 
\begin_inset Formula $t\ge0$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left(1/R\right)_{t\wedge\tau_{n}}\xrightarrow[n\to\infty]{}\left(1/R\right)_{t},
\]

\end_inset

as a consequence of the stopping times diverging to infinity almost surely 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau_{n}\to\infty$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now we would like to take 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}$
\end_inset

 on both sides.
 Because each stopped process is a martingale,
 its mean is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{E}\left(1/R_{t\wedge\tau_{n}}\right)=1,\quad\text{for all }n.
\]

\end_inset

But if we let 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 tend to infinity and so 
\begin_inset Formula $1/R_{t\wedge\tau_{n}}$
\end_inset

 reaches 
\begin_inset Formula $1/R_{t}$
\end_inset

 almost surely,
 we have no guarantee that the expectation preserves that limit.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
A great counterexample is the function sequence 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{n}(\omega)=n\boldsymbol{1}_{(0,1/n)}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega=[0,1]$
\end_inset

.
 On each 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{n}(\omega)$
\end_inset

 tends to zero,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{n}\to0$
\end_inset

 almost surely.
 But while all the functions have a unit mean 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}X_{n}=1$
\end_inset

,
 the limit is the absolute zero.
 Almost sure convergence is not strong enough to carry the 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}$
\end_inset

 in the limit,
 as the functions 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{n}$
\end_inset

 are explodingly tall on small but non-neglectible sets 
\begin_inset Formula $(0,1/n)$
\end_inset

.
 Instead,
 in a distribution sense 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{n}$
\end_inset

 converges to the Dirak-delta 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{0}$
\end_inset

,
 which has a unit expectation just like every 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{n}$
\end_inset

.
 The difference between 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{0}$
\end_inset

 and the pointwise limit 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 is only at the origin where 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{0}$
\end_inset

 is conceptually 
\begin_inset Formula $\infty$
\end_inset

,
 and where the mass of all 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{n}$
\end_inset

 leaks in the limit.
 Depending on the choice of topology,
 we get two different limits.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In our local martingale situation,
 
\begin_inset Formula $1/R_{t}$
\end_inset

 is just the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

wrong
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 limit of the two.
 Let us decompose the stopped processes into their explosive and nice parts:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{align*}
1/R_{t\wedge\tau_{n}} & =1/R_{t}\cdot\boldsymbol{1}_{t\le\tau_{n}}+1/R_{\tau_{n}}\cdot\boldsymbol{1}_{t>\tau_{n}}\\
 & =1/R_{t}\cdot\boldsymbol{1}_{t\le\tau_{n}}+n\boldsymbol{1}_{t>\tau_{n}}
\end{align*}

\end_inset

 and take expectations 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
1=\mathbb{E}\left(1/R_{t}\cdot\boldsymbol{1}_{t\le\tau_{n}}\right)+n\mathbb{P}(t>\tau_{n}).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If we let 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 to infinity in that decomposition,
 the truncated 
\begin_inset Formula $1/R_{t}\cdot\boldsymbol{1}_{t\le\tau_{n}}$
\end_inset

 converges almost surely to 
\begin_inset Formula $1/R_{t}$
\end_inset

 
\emph on
monotonically,

\emph default
 and so we can take the limit inside the expectation:
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{E}\left(1/R_{t}\cdot\boldsymbol{1}_{t\le\tau_{n}}\right)\to\mathbb{E}(1/R_{t})$
\end_inset

.
 So we get a tangible expression for the limit expectation 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mathbb{E}(1/R_{t})=1-\lim_{n}n\cdot\mathbb{P}(t>\tau_{n}).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here Chatgpt says that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mathbb{P}(t>\tau_{n})$
\end_inset

 does not die out faster than 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

,
 so the limit is nonzero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So our stopped processes are like the 
\begin_inset Formula $n\boldsymbol{1}_{(0,1/n)}$
\end_inset

 functions – 
\begin_inset Formula $1/R_{t\wedge\tau_{n}}$
\end_inset

 converge to their unstopped parent 
\begin_inset Formula $1/R_{t}$
\end_inset

,
 but only 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega$
\end_inset

-pointwise,
 with small parts of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 on which 
\begin_inset Formula $1/R_{t}$
\end_inset

 explode exactly so that their average over the whole of 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

 is 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The event 
\begin_inset Formula $t>\tau_{n}$
\end_inset

 shrinks to the empty event in the limit,
 but we can imagine that an outcome of positive probability sits there in some larger space,
 and the extra bit of expectation leaks there so that the total expectation is still 
\begin_inset Formula $1$
\end_inset

 and the expectation over the rest (the original 
\begin_inset Formula $\Omega$
\end_inset

) has to be strictly less.
\end_layout

